nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127032" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127032" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/95e7e2df02e4694d85be868f348bb65fb6a7bf2c646b89000b9af655f937a12f.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n- The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative\n- The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero then the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nFor an arbitrary closed curve, the outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. From the diagram below it's clear that there is a net flow *out* through $C.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/bfd6a4f3d368bc5920e1c79eed913f4ef21fcdf8b9eb42bbc5ce3f5c2ba7d36b.png)\n\nTherefore, the flux is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/789b387e512c53895b0cfa499c70547046318a29da6d9af7f556a07bcac830ed.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $F$ across $-C$ is positive, and we must have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{-C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127025" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127025" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=15xy\\,i+5{x}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{5}\\,i+\\frac{y}{5}\\,j.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/d3d68329d81150c4a975bae4963d4e480cefaef0f2a0a29a0648c3793e9fb462.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(15xy\\,i+5{x}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{5}\\,i+\\frac{y}{5}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{2}y+{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $4{x}^{2}y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127056" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2xy+{x}^{2}y"] ["math" "3x{y}^{2}"] ["math" "3x{y}^{2}+x"] ["math" "4{x}^{2}y"] ["math" "xy+3x{y}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127056" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{x}^{2}y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2{x}^{2}\\,i+2{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(2{x}^{2}\\,i+2{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}+{y}^{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127049" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}"] ["math" "{x}^{4}{y}^{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127049" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=x\\,i+y\\,j.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/386f010932b5846e7f47a605a94a5b5d580bd35dbe37512b1583d15d29f3dc70.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (xi+y\\,j)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${x}^{3}-{y}^{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-126833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x-y"] ["math" "{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}{y}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126833" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{3}-{y}^{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j$ and the circle $C$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n=\\frac{x}{2}\\,i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(4{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,i+2{x}^{3}y\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127057" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127057" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}x"] ["math" "3x+{y}^{3}"] ["math" "3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}"] ["math" "6x{y}^{2}"] ["math" "x{y}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127057" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/4f8af75aece2346ffbf7a09e1ee90b6e0270f5661dd74e068abb2b2a848e5b97.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)={x}^{3}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,j$ and the square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ shown above. If the flux of $F$ across the side ${C}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nIn our case, we need to find an expression for the flux across only the part ${C}_{2}$ of the closed curve $C.$ Since this side is perpendicular to the $y$-axis and we require an outward-pointing unit normal vector, we must have $n=j.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/a83c3476bbe2ed2872ed8938dda59c5d0e6d58d05ecaf662748e7a184a4994a4.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}({x}^{3}\\,i+3{y}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $3{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2x{y}^{2}"] ["math" "3{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{y}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/70a51bcda43cd04a56c6b87a10723258c995e8daae87689a2ae8af5f44112ead.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i+y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot 8=32.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126890" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-126890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "32"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "64"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126890" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/ea53225d28411f38006861d6c8dc46f8d3d1ea01b3bb6183954f68e7124c5682.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=3\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=3\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127190" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127190" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/8e1767b9bea45e742df4953f0ec50b2d43c9622994035342307dc2be6abba8de.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/878a9992405e5968a03a04d12f42aeb85bca52c07dc7081244b04011643a74bf.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=-2\\,i$ is parallel to ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{4},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net inflow through ${C}_{1}$ will cancel with the net outflow through ${C}_{3}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127194" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-6"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "12"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127194" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/19fca0188077e56d6b56048d1cd48ea840922cafe7873aaa02937c8c2845e998.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nNow, since the vector field $F=2\\,j$ is parallel to ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3},$ there is zero net flow through these two sides. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for the flux reduces to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nNow, since the vector field is uniform, the net outflow through ${C}_{2}$ will cancel with the net inflow through ${C}_{4}.$ That is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s=-{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s\\,⟹\\,{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-126881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-126881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/fdb05b3fcbfcfdab8344eaaaf54584a95f20696618b412873ddeb219b7a401b3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3bd953b7fb02b90a55a29bea6f58ff9bd8bab77d62b1df1c547ca899656d37a3.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2(2-(-2)) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides ${C}_{2},{C}_{3},$ and ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=4\\cdot (-8)=-32.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127198" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-14"] ["math" "-16"] ["math" "-28"] ["math" "-32"] ["math" "-8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127198" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-32"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/0ac0fa01b3e09d268115a604a53ca6247c9e26d88603482223aa43927df20561.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=-2x\\,i+5y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $2$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/29f4b57751e27f01539fb48acf115b6e255d47a04ee647ef85aa496731b266c8.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(-2x\\,i+5y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(-2x\\,i+5y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=20.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot (-8)+2\\cdot 20=24.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226152" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-226152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "24"] ["math" "32"] ["math" "36"] ["math" "40"] ["math" "48"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226152" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/6696cf798bb4ecdf8c6eaeb65b0bb49477b04207c22bceff1e2cdee8614b1728.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=x\\,i-2y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $2$ and height $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/aba4a2e6603e65d3f67e65c5af73b2cdda6bdf798d9e9d09005f738d700500ea.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=4.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(x\\,i-2y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+2\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot (-8)=-8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-16"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/ec48931b53248128f4eefd2fe2428120adeef983b6fd2a613e7dcca96c00fe12.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i-y\\,j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/867ab8337894fa75d010bcb12557702059a4a93aaa2bdef2f853c46ff2801d67.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=3\\,i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=36.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(2x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+3\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-3}^{3}3\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-18.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 36+2\\cdot (-18)=36.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-226135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-work