n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(3x\\,i-y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 6+2\\cdot (-2)=8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127200" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-127200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-16"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "16"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-127200" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/0a62dfaec4e967433ef56d319e4e66a220441a711ce97a84ca96a4bbd7d0bf37.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=2x\\,i-3y\\,j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides ${C}_{i}$ and unit vectors ${n}_{i}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/00a9df76d0be8ffd33b2ee968b57a21eb1e6947af6e94505313f163b584f5769.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side ${C}_{1}.$ We have that ${n}_{1}=i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=2i+t\\,j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=j,\\,t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{3}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot {n}_{3}\\,\\text{d}s=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side ${C}_{2}.$ We have that ${n}_{2}=j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(2x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nr(t)=t\\,i+j,\\,⇒\\,{r}^{′}(t)=i,\\,t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side ${C}_{4}$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{4}}F\\cdot {n}_{4}\\,\\text{d}s=-12.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=2\\cdot 8+2\\cdot (-12)=-8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "485be7eeba5f00230c61a0a8a371da9224fbe5b39b1332e31db7d55e5d8cae02"} {"question" "q-226149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10"] ["math" "-12"] ["math" "-16"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "q-226149" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/state.json" "file_sha256" "e821c4741cfee5d3ff2b640a12a120f90c8ce85b383d6ca0b868bbe890b83df9"} {"question" "e-15147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero\n\n- The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/7e7fbe6aaf1310b186344a3cf5a127075f11a86b51db2e687ddc7035d5d890f8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/example-15147.json" "file_sha256" "d669fb4eaccb3305238c2b4fcdaec3a586a438358f1d5521e01a8d240ef98507"} {"question" "e-15147" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C\\mathbin:$ \n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/a72dc9866562a42a49590fd169424307675b2c14b36833bd729026f46b677eec.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/3a6ac8656b2e0b5cc482a16eb3a4fab57cb681c9a40cf634da47f9a4323d9fdf.png)\n\nThe outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf n$ at every point on $C.$ Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/4a3d9864bb6bada1fd218b299427bc5bb4ffcbcccb1a504fff77416d5d292151.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/9310d17389f61de2984c0e23cfe1060e77d4a87a140d2f1c892bdbab9b99629b.png)\n\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/example-15147.json" "file_sha256" "d669fb4eaccb3305238c2b4fcdaec3a586a438358f1d5521e01a8d240ef98507"} {"question" "e-15148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 8y\\,\\mathbf i + 4x\\,\\mathbf j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^2+y^2 = 4.$ If the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/707f56c886072458eefdd7bcee2328992caa214b4f4954ba59c92bc972b66409.png)\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf n = \\dfrac x2\\,\\mathbf i + \\dfrac y2\\,\\mathbf j.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/example-15148.json" "file_sha256" "e05ffb35bb3a0e3903967d747a72fe2ace471f2beb395d237b2f1eeee6c688ad"} {"question" "e-15148" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/ff84680c55f5349431cf3d70066eeaba2c680569faa3b2210e5d14ef63ccd99e.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}(8y\\,i+4x\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i+\\frac{y}{2}\\,j)\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}4xy+2xy\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}6xy\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $6xy.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/example-15148.json" "file_sha256" "e05ffb35bb3a0e3903967d747a72fe2ace471f2beb395d237b2f1eeee6c688ad"} {"question" "e-15149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/05c91d681a0c84bbe9d8b6cab9bd8cdf1dca8bf88c851b8c7a7045f73ff9844c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown below. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/example-15149.json" "file_sha256" "90ae2b69db1c8cf7d09b13968159710f995c625c2d347b18361c1e8ea890d846"} {"question" "e-15149" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/1de4c4c55784b4a84a0493a4cc65082c430cadcc39c3d808001b2fd27665e968.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(2x\\,i+2y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3,$ and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/example-15149.json" "file_sha256" "90ae2b69db1c8cf7d09b13968159710f995c625c2d347b18361c1e8ea890d846"} {"question" "e-15150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/59b5f1676f036f80e6aa2e9e331327ed9ec28e88bacda52a91ee0865736e9ce3.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 5x\\,\\mathbf i - 3y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$ \n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/example-15150.json" "file_sha256" "a96bacfb47f24cab1155e5e0222f8abadb034c09af90be0b43209c00538c249a"} {"question" "e-15150" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s\n=\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n+\n\\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/assets/793755cda5243dbb427d8722c21ab150a4821c3957f0ccd0ef0fedbbd576e924.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages. \n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}(5x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot i\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}x\\,\\text{d}s & =5{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20.\n$$\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}(5x\\,i-3y\\,j)\\cdot j\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}y\\,\\text{d}s & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}1\\cdot ||{r}^{′}(t)||\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{-2}^{2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14061989/example-15150.json" "file_sha256" "a96bacfb47f24cab1155e5e0222f8abadb034c09af90be0b43209c00538c249a"}] "usage" {"e-15149" [] "q-126881" [] "e-15150" [] "e-15148" [] "q-126728" [] "q-126833" [] "e-15147" []} "basis" 1977 "retractions" [[17592186179963 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/q-126728.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is positive\n2. The flux of $F$ across $C$ is zero\n3. The flux of $F$ across $-C$ is negative"] [17592186179965 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x, y) = x^{2}i - y^{2}j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^{2} + y^{2} = 1$. If the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$∮_{C}AAAAAAAds$,\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $n = xi + yj$.*"] [17592186179967 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/q-126881.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F = 2j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ across $C$."] [17592186179408 :question/problem "Consider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F$ shown below. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction.\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is negative\n2. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero\n3. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is positive\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/0fc4f6ac431f4a118406f367a2049f25.png)"] [17592186179408 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\nTo understand the flux intuitively, we imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a stream of flowing water. The flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ represents the net flow of water escaping the region bounded by $C\\mathbin$:\n\n- If more water flows out through $C$ than in through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *out* through $C$), the flux is *positive*.\n- If more water flows in through $C$ than out through $C$ (i.e., there is a net flow *in* through $C$), the flux is *negative*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/ceedec3a772973eda45dc8e75ea8a1b4.png)\n\n- If the net flow through $C$ is zero, the flux equals zero.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/7d3a45be0fad34026a756e0685ef72ed.png)\n\nThe outward-pointing unit normal vector tells us which direction is \"out.\"\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. From the diagram below we see that $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to $\\mathbf n$ at every point on $C$. Therefore, there is no net flow of $\\mathbf F$ through $C$.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/f3f7b1aa12b9af600ca4107f8ba7963e.png)\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is zero, and we must have\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0$.\n- Statement III is false. When measuring flux through a closed curve, the orientation of the curve does not matter because the outward-pointing unit normal vector is the same at each point on $C$ in either case.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/f7f6796435787eeb8416a755b211bebc.png)\nSo, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $-C$ is zero, and we must have\n$\\oint\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186179409 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = 8y\\,\\mathbf i + 4x\\,\\mathbf j$ and the circle $C$ given by $x^2+y^2 = 4$. If the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$AAAAAAA$\n\nwhat is the missing expression?\n\n*Hint: The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$ is $\\mathbf n = \\dfrac x2\\,\\mathbf i + \\dfrac y2\\,\\mathbf j$.*"] [17592186179409 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/abb3c6599e722f86db8e3d48613846f1.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)(8yi + 4xj) \\cdot (\\frac{x}{2}i + \\frac{y}{2}j)ds \\\\\n&= ∮_(C)4xy + 2xyds \\\\\n&= ∮_(C)6xyds\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $6xy$."] [17592186179410 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 2x\\,\\mathbf i + 2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O$, as shown below. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/4ba14e820f544a5113eaea401b1ac37f.png)\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across all four sides of the square.*"] [17592186179410 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/16258660947a1a027a68b4d411919586.png)\n\nFirst, consider the side $C_1$. We have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{C_{1}}F \\cdot n_{1}ds &= ∫_{C_{1}}(2xi + 2yj) \\cdot ids \\\\\n&= 2∫_{C_{1}}xds\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1]\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2∫_{C_{1}}xds &= 2∫_{-1}^{1}1 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt \\\\\n&= 2∫_{-1}^{1}dt \\\\\n&= 2(1 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integrals over the sides $C_2, C_3$, and $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = 4\\cdot 4 = 16\n$$"] [17592186179411 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = 5x\\,\\mathbf i - 3y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a rectangle $C$ with base $4$ and height $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/14fbee42939abe16d9fd3f09b62b7242.png)\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*"] [17592186179411 :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n$\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s$,\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$. In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s + \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3715/Source/Images/8550241bbdc42d8ce5e3d1b6f1658d69.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1$. We have that $\\mathbf n_1 = \\mathbf i$, and therefore,\n$∫_{C_{1}}F \\cdot n_{1}ds|= ∫_{C_{1}}(5xi - 3yj) \\cdot ids; = 5∫_{C_{1}}xds$.\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1$, we have\n$\\mathbf r(t) = 2\\mathbf i + t\\,\\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [-1,1]$.\nTherefore,\n$5∫_{C_{1}}xds|= 5∫_{-1}^{1}2 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt; = 10∫_{-1}^{1}dt; = 10 \\cdot 2; = 20$.\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n$\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_1 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_3} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_3 \\: \\textrm{d}s = 20$.\n\n- Next, we consider the side $C_2$. We have that $\\mathbf n_2 = \\mathbf j$, and therefore,\n$∫_{C_{2}}F \\cdot n_{2}ds|= ∫_{C_{2}}(5xi - 3yj) \\cdot jds; =-3∫_{C_{2}}yds$.\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2$, we have\n$\\mathbf r(t) = t\\,\\mathbf i + \\mathbf j, \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\mathbf r'(t) = \\mathbf i, \\qquad t\\in [-2,2]$.\nTherefore,\n$-3∫_{C_{2}}yds|=-3∫_{-2}^{2}1 \\cdot \\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid dt; =-3∫_{-2}^{2}dt; = (-3) \\cdot 4; =-12$.\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n$\\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_2 \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C_4} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n}_4 \\: \\textrm{d}s = -12$.\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\textrm d s= 2 \\cdot 20 + 2 \\cdot (-12) = 16\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186177779 17592186177780 17592186177781 17592186177782 17592186177783 17592186177791 17592186177792 17592186177793 17592186177794 17592186177795 17592186177803 17592186177804 17592186177805 17592186177806 17592186177807] "immutable_fields" [[17592186177778 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177778 :answer-field/id] [17592186177778 :answer-field/key] [17592186177778 :answer-field/type] [17592186177790 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177790 :answer-field/id] [17592186177790 :answer-field/key] [17592186177790 :answer-field/type] [17592186177802 :answer-field/correct] [17592186177802 :answer-field/id] [17592186177802 :answer-field/key] [17592186177802 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
